LIBPKGCHECK-EXEC(7)

# NAME

libpkgcheck-exec - native check execution adapter

# DESCRIPTION

*libpkgcheck-exec* admits concrete source, built-package, logical check-input,
root-view, and temporary resources for one sealed *libpkgcheck* request. It
translates the source-owned check program into a backend-neutral *libpkgexec*
request with purpose *check*, invokes one backend, and seals terminal
*libpkgcheck* evidence.

*seal_execution_request()* reproduces the exact request without filesystem
effects. *prepare()* uses that request and adds only the concrete call-scoped
resource materializations.

Check-input resources are matched by exact *pkgbuild::build_input_identity* and
retained in the canonical order of the sealed request. Caller order is not
semantically significant. Missing, duplicate, and unrelated logical input
authorities are rejected.

Each input carries a separate concrete *pkgexec::resource_identity* and host
path. The adapter does not issue or verify a content-addressed package-tree
identity. Paths are operational coordinates, not package authority.

Concrete source, package, input, and temporary paths must be absolute, non-root,
and mutually disjoint after lexical normalization. Distinct admitted semantic
resources must not share one concrete resource identity. The root-view path is
a separate namespace coordinate and may be the filesystem root.

Check programs receive *PKG_SOURCE_ROOT=/check/source* for the exact admitted
read-only source resource and *PKG_PACKAGE_ROOT=/check/package* for the
sealed checked package. *PKG_CHECK_INPUT_ROOT=/check/inputs* names the logical
check-input namespace and *PKG_CHECK_INPUTS* lists its canonical package-name
children in sealed request order. *PKG_JOBS* projects the parallelism already
admitted by the checked build policy. Recipes do not consume opaque build-input
identities as path names. No distribution-branded check aliases are exported.

# DURABLE EVIDENCE

The versioned check-execution codec retains the exact *libpkgexec* execution
record and adapter-owned terminal outcome, failure classification, and evidence
identities. Decode requires the exact check request, execution request, and
backend profile bodies. It does not reconstruct authorities from identity
strings and performs no filesystem or execution effect.

# AUTHORITY

The library does not acquire, unpack, or materialize package resources,
perform process syscalls, schedule transaction nodes, or publish controller
progression.

# SEE ALSO

*pkgcheck_exec_result_codec*(3), *libpkgcheck*(3), *libpkgexec*(3)
